---
metaTitle: Grading standards | The 1 to 70 scale, designations and ancients
metaDescription: >-
  How coins are graded: the 1 to 70 scale, details grades and designations, how
  ancient coins are graded with strike and surface, and what the label shows.
eyebrow: Grading standards
heading: The grading scale.
intro: >-
  A grade sums up a coin's state of preservation in a single figure that
  collectors everywhere can compare. This is how the scale works, and how it is
  applied here.
scale:
  - range: '1'
    code: PO
    name: Poor
    text: Barely identifiable as to type; the date may be worn away.
  - range: '2'
    code: FR
    name: Fair
    text: The type can be identified, but much of the design and lettering is worn smooth.
  - range: '3'
    code: AG
    name: About Good
    text: Heavily worn, with the rims worn into the legends in places.
  - range: 4–6
    code: G
    name: Good
    text: Heavily worn. The main design is outlined and the rims are mostly complete.
  - range: 8–10
    code: VG
    name: Very Good
    text: Well worn, with the main features clear and some interior detail remaining.
  - range: 12–15
    code: F
    name: Fine
    text: Moderate, even wear. All major features are sharp.
  - range: 20–35
    code: VF
    name: Very Fine
    text: Light to moderate wear on the high points; finer details are visible.
  - range: 40–45
    code: XF
    name: Extremely Fine
    text: Light wear on the highest points only. Traces of mint luster may remain.
  - range: 50–58
    code: AU
    name: About Uncirculated
    text: A trace of wear on the high points, with much of the luster intact.
  - range: 60–70
    code: MS
    name: Mint State
    text: >-
      No wear at all. The number reflects marks, luster, strike and eye appeal; a
      70 shows no post-production imperfections under magnification.
sections:
  - mark: grade
    title: Where the numbers come from
    text: |-
      The 1 to 70 scale began in 1949, when the American numismatist William Sheldon proposed it for large cents. It has since become the common language of grading for milled coins, used by collectors, dealers and grading services alike.

      Proof coins, struck specially for collectors, are graded on the same numbers, from Proof 60 to Proof 70.
  - mark: review
    title: Details grades
    text: >-
      A coin can be genuine and still have a problem that affects it, such as
      cleaning, tooling, environmental damage, a repair or heavy scratches. Such a
      coin may be described with a details grade: the grade its wear corresponds
      to, together with a plain note of the problem, in place of a numeric grade.
  - mark: aperture
    title: Designations
    text: >-
      Some coins call for more than a number. Designations describe qualities the
      grade alone does not capture, such as the color of copper (red, red-brown or
      brown), mirror-like prooflike surfaces, the frosted contrast of cameo proofs,
      or a strike sharp enough to show full detail on its highest points.
  - mark: laurel
    title: How ancient coins are graded
    text: |-
      Ancient coins were struck by hand, one at a time, from hand-cut dies. Their centering, the state of the dies and the flans they were struck on vary from coin to coin, so a scale built for machine-struck coinage does not describe them well.

      Ancient coins therefore receive a descriptive grade, from Good through Very Fine and Extremely Fine to About Uncirculated and Mint State, together with two subgrades on a scale of one to five: one for strike (centering, sharpness and the state of the dies) and one for surface (the condition of the metal itself). At the services that include it, the coin's weight is recorded and its identification is expanded.
  - mark: label
    title: What the label shows
    text: >-
      Each label records the certificate number, the coin's attribution and its
      grade, with any designation. For ancient coins it also shows the strike and
      surface subgrades and, where the service includes it, the weight. A
      pedigree can be added on request.
  - mark: balance
    title: Consistency
    text: >-
      Grading is a matter of trained judgement. Our aim is simple: the same coin
      should receive the same grade whenever it is examined. Every grade is
      reviewed before the coin is sealed.
---
